    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make an issue of the panel being all men**, at least not formally or confrontationally. However, it does offer some clues about the organization's culture and the hiring dynamics at play.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what it signals and how to evaluate the situation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does it indicate gender discrimination against you?\n* **Legally and formally:** No. Because you and the successful candidate are both men, the all-male panel did not disadvantage you on the basis of sex. Raising panel gender composition as a grievance would almost certainly be dismissed and could damage your professional reputation in the local engineering community.\n* **Industry context:** Structural engineering leadership historically skews male. While best-practice organizations now mandate mixed-gender or cross-disciplinary interview panels to reduce groupthink, an all-male panel of four senior leaders is still common in traditional consultancies.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What might the panel composition and outcome actually signal?\nWhile not gender discrimination against you, the panel setup and the announced rationale point to several common hiring patterns:\n\n* **Affinity and \"Culture Fit\" Bias:** Homogeneous panels (same gender, similar background, common peer group) are notoriously prone to affinity bias. They tend to favor candidates who feel familiar or whom existing leaders personally like and feel comfortable around.\n* **Commercial Value vs. Technical Seniority:** \n  * You brought **15 years of design experience, a master's degree, and three major project leads** (strong technical and project leadership).\n  * The selected candidate brought **8 years and \"excellent client relationships\"**.\n  * For a *Head of Group* role, consultancies frequently prioritize **business development, fee generation, and client retention** over technical capability. An 8-year engineer who is charismatic with developers, architects, and contractors will often beat out a more technically experienced engineer if the firm needs revenue growth.\n* **Salary or Hierarchy Considerations:** A candidate with 8 years of experience may command a lower salary package and be more deferential to upper management than a 15-year veteran with established convictions.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why haven't they responded to your feedback request?\nTwo weeks of silence after requesting *written* feedback is frustrating, but typical:\n* **Legal caution:** Consultancies and HR departments are often wary of putting rejection feedback in writing, fearing it could be used to challenge the decision.\n* **Deprioritization:** Once the role is filled, internal priorities shift to onboarding and project work; candidate debriefs get pushed aside.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Do not mention the panel’s gender:** Bringing this up will sound like an unfocused grievance rather than a legitimate professional critique.\n2. **Follow up for feedback—offer a phone call:** People are far more candid verbally than in writing. Send a polite, forward-looking note:\n   > *\"I know the team is busy, but if a brief 10-minute phone call is easier than a written response, I'd welcome any constructive feedback on areas where I can strengthen my profile for future leadership roles.\"*\n3. **Ask the right questions if you get the call:**\n   * Specifically probe the commercial expectations: *\"Was the panel looking for a stronger business development track record or specific client relationships?\"*\n4. **Use it as cultural data:** If this firm operates as an insular \"old boys' club\" that doesn't respect candidates enough to provide post-interview closure, it may not have been an environment where your leadership style would have thrived anyway.\n",
